Image 1
The Buddhist section deploys a three-bay altar configuration housed within a single hall, with an extraordinary architectural ceiling above. Three separate niche altars are contained within a unified dark-wood carved canopy structure:
LEFT BAY        CENTRE BAY          RIGHT BAY
金钱伯殿           地藏王菩薩           大二爷伯
(Wealth God   (Dizang Bodhisattva) (Tua Di Ya Pek - Hei Bai Wu Chang)
in the underworld)                    
Reading from Image 1, two plaques are clearly legible:

Centre: 地藏王 (Dìzàng Wáng) — "Kṣitigarbha King"
Right: 大二爷伯 (Da Er Ye Bo) — "Tua Di Ya Pek"

The three-bay structure gives each altar equal architectural framing — same niche format, same canopy, same red background. Visual equality of three distinct iconographic-theological functions is encoded in the three-bay format, mirroring the bilateral equality of the two subsidiary altars in the Taoist main hall analyzed previously.
Above the altar structure, visible at top of Image 1: the circular carved dome of the caisson ceiling, which centers the entire hall on the Dizang altar below it.
Central altar — Dizang Bodhisattva (地藏王菩薩)
Image 2
ICON₁ — Dizang Bodhisattva (地藏菩薩, Kṣitigarbha)
ICON₁ → ⟨ central, dominant, front-facing,
           soteriological and intercessory ⟩

Iconographic features:
  - Seated in lotus posture on lotus throne
  - Wears bodhisattva crown (五佛冠 five-Buddha crown)
  - Holds a khakkara staff (錫杖) — the ringed staff whose
    sound wakes the sleeping in the six realms of existence
  - Pink/gold coloring
  - Flanked by two attendants
  - Three smaller figures on lower shelf directly in front

Canonical vow:
  "If hell is not empty, I will not attain Buddhahood;
   only when all sentient beings are liberated will I
   myself achieve Bodhi" (地獄不空，誓不成佛)
  — the most radical salvific vow in the Mahāyāna corpus,
    specifically directed at those suffering in the
    underworld courts visible in the right bay
ICON₂ — Left attendant (dark-robed figure)
ICON₂ → ⟨ paired-lateral, subordinate, front-facing,
           protective and administrative ⟩

Features:
  - Dark complexion, dark robes
  - Elaborate headdress
  - Holds implements (possibly a scroll or register)
  - Standing posture

Provisional identification:
  Possibly 道明和尚 (Daoming Monk) — though his typical
  depiction is as a young monk, some regional variants
  show this attendant with darker/more austere appearance.
  Alternatively, a Hell Guardian figure associated with
  Dizang's underworld administration.

  Flagged for fieldwork confirmation.
ICON₃ — Right attendant (bearded elder)
ICON₃ → ⟨ paired-lateral, subordinate, front-facing,
           intercessory ⟩

Features:
  - Elderly male figure with long white/grey beard
  - Gold/beige robes
  - Holds a scroll or ruyi scepter

Provisional identification: 閔公 (Mǐn Gōng)
  — the elderly nobleman whose land became the site
    of Dizang's monastery in the founding legend,
    and who eventually ordained alongside his son
    under Dizang's teaching
  — canonical member of the Dizang triad
Lower shelf — three smaller figures:
Three smaller figures are positioned on a lower shelf
directly in front of the main Dizang configuration.
These are consistent with additional attendant deities
or protector figures of Dizang's administrative entourage.
Requires closer fieldwork documentation.
