Left bay — provisionally unidentified deity
Image 3
Plaque: 金钱伯殿 (Jīn Bó Yín)
ICON → ⟨ central within niche, singular,
          front-facing, devotional ⟩

Iconographic features:
  - Standing figure
  - Elaborate crown/headdress with coral-branch elements
  - Long black hair flowing to both sides
  - Gold/metallic robes
  - Surrounded by elaborate gold relief carvings against
    red background — auspicious cloud formations, gold
    ingots, coral, and other wealth-auspicious objects
  - Smaller attendant figure on lower shelf below

Analytical note:
  The wealth-auspicious iconographic background (ingots,
  coral, gold cloud formations) suggests a deity associated
  with prosperity, material abundance, or the afterlife
  economy (冥府財神 — Underworld Wealth Deity).

  The placement adjacent to Dizang and the Tua Di Ya Pek
  raises the possibility that this is a deity who oversees
  the ritual economy of paper offerings burned for the dead —
  specifically the deity who receives and distributes the
  金紙銀紙 (gold and silver paper money) that mourners burn
  to provide for deceased relatives in the underworld.

  PROVISIONAL: cannot be definitively identified from
  visual analysis alone. Consultation with temple management
  is required.
